T = {
    "705": "CHAPTER 5\n\nDiscussion: Processual Theoretical Elaboration of the Seizing Mechanism",

    "706": "The purpose of this chapter is fundamentally to reinterpret and refine the theoretical logic by transposing the empirical clues excavated through the multi-layered case analysis of the preceding Chapter 4 into the rigorous theoretical grammar of Organizational Dynamic Capabilities Theory, and more specifically, the Seizing mechanism. In the foregoing Chapter 4, a single case was dissected in depth in which, despite the firm gestation of an organizational-level normative judgment directed toward field-level AI utilization training needs, the pertinent agenda item ultimately failed to leap onto the trajectory of formal decision-making and instead descended into acute stagnation.",

    "707": "Through the trajectory of such meticulous tracing, this study has confirmed that the fatal discontinuity interposed between 'judgment' gestated within the larger system and 'decision' capable of driving it toward concrete execution is by no means an accidental byproduct arising from simple transient administrative delays or the passive attitudes of particular members. Rather, this study has clearly demonstrated that such an obstinate void constitutes the manifest expression of a processual bottleneck structurally gestated and ossified in the deep strata of the Seizing stage posited by Dynamic Capabilities Theory.",

    "708": "Figure 4.2 synthesizes these case analysis results and visually presents the structure in which, although a judgment regarding the necessity of AI utilization was formed within the organization (confirming judgment formation at the Sensing dimension) and formal approval was reached, formal decision-making was not generated at the Seizing stage due to the ambiguity of responsible agents, the indeterminacy of budgets and schedules, the non-placement of the item on the strategic agenda, and the non-initiation of follow-up procedures. This figure constitutes the empirical point of departure from which the theoretical discussion of this chapter commences. The diagram demonstrates that the state in which no decision occurred after judgment is not a simple void but an organizational outcome that has stabilized as a non-event.",

    "709": "Taking this empirical structure presented in Figure 4.2 as its point of departure, this chapter moves beyond prior approaches that have understood Seizing as a singular act of choice or a visible decision-making event, and instead focuses on the 'conditions for process initiation' and 'structural mechanisms' required for judgment to be translated into institutional decision. Through this, the present study seeks to theoretically explain that the Seizing stage can persist within an organization not merely as a 'case of failure,' but as a state of 'non-initiation' of the mechanism itself.",

    "710": "Such theoretical reconstruction contributes to explaining, beyond the specific case of AI utilization training, the more general organizational phenomenon in which organizations form judgments about environmental changes yet fail to convert them into strategic action. In the following section, each bottleneck element included in Figure 4.1 is decomposed and interpreted at the constitutive dimension of the Seizing mechanism, thereby analyzing the process by which the judgment\u2013decision-making conversion stagnates in more precise theoretical language.",

    "711": "Accordingly, this chapter is not one that evaluates whether Seizing has succeeded or failed, but one that elucidates, at the level of processual mechanism, under what conditions the process by which judgment is translated into decision fails to be initiated.",

    "712": "5.1. Theoretical Re-positioning of the Empirical Findings",

    "713": "The purpose of this section is not to merely summarize the empirical findings derived from Chapter 4 as case-level results. Rather, by repositioning the core phenomenon identified in this study within the context of existing theoretical discourse, its aim is to clarify what theoretical implications the findings hold for organizational theory and strategic research. In particular, this study does not interpret the phenomenon in which, despite the formation of judgment regarding the necessity of AI utilization within the organization, said judgment failed to convert into formal decision-making, as a result of performance failure or implementation deficiency. Instead, this phenomenon is reconceptualized as processual stagnation\u2014a state in which the Seizing stage of Dynamic Capabilities Theory has failed to actually operate, meaning that judgment has not been translated into decision (Teece, 2007; Teece, 2014). The processual stagnation thus defined in this study does not signify mere delay or inefficiency; rather, it denotes a state in which the institutional translation of judgment\u2014the core constitutive element of the Seizing stage\u2014has not occurred: that is, judgment formed within the organization has not been converted into the institutional decision-making language of formal agenda-setting, assignment of responsible personnel, schedule establishment, and procedure initiation.",

    "714": "The theoretical re-positioning work developed in this section is constructed upon the firm foundation of the operational indicators rigorously stipulated in the preceding Chapter 3. Specifically, whether a judgment regarding the subject agenda was gestated, and whether it transitioned into substantive decision-making, was determined with the thorough exclusion of the researcher's arbitrary and subjective inference. Instead, only empirically observable concrete indicators were employed as criteria, including official documentary records, electronic approval system clearance, formal committee agenda-setting status, explicit assignment of responsible agents, confirmation of specific deadlines, and initiation of follow-up planning. Consequently, the theoretical concept of 'processual stagnation' newly proposed by this study is not a product of arbitrary interpretation, but a product of logical verification derived from meticulous cross-referencing of field-level empirical raw data against operational indicators, conducted upon a rigorously controlled methodological foundation.",

    "715": "The trajectory of the multi-layered case dissection developed in the foregoing Chapter 4 starkly exposes the following highly particular organizational state. Within the observed ecosystem, a positive consensus regarding the justification for AI technology adoption was formed and preserved for a considerable period; moreover, by fully traversing the established administrative channels, it acquired the firm status of 'institutional judgment.' Nevertheless, in the trajectory following such formal approval, the institutionalized decision-making phases that ought to have ensued\u2014formal agenda-setting, assignment of responsible personnel, coordination of implementation timetables, and initiation of follow-up procedures\u2014were never opened. What must be captured with utmost acuity here is the fact that, even within this obstinate deadlock, no explicit resistance attempting to reject or wholly negate the matter was observed whatsoever. In sum, the essence of the problem excavated from the field lies not in a 'flawed judgment containing errors,' but in the fatal discontinuity that the 'procedural transit pathway itself, which should have driven the gestated judgment into a full decision, fundamentally ceased to operate.' This strongly suggests that the larger system successfully arrived at forging a rational judgment, yet the translation mechanism for incorporating it into the structure of substantive formal decision ultimately failed to awaken. Such an obstinate terrain of non-activation has been repeatedly confirmed through the process tracing analysis of the preceding chapter and the diagram (Figure 4.2) that three-dimensionally visualizes it.",

    "716": "These findings form an important contrast with the analytical perspective that prior Seizing research has presupposed. Previous studies have conceptualized Seizing as explicit decision-making acts such as strategic choice, resource allocation, and investment decisions undertaken after opportunity recognition (Eisenhardt & Martin, 2000; Teece, 2007). In this approach, whether 'a decision was made' and 'whether that decision was successful' have generally been the center of analysis. However, the case of this study demonstrates that, prior to the question of whether a decision succeeded, the process can stagnate at the stage before a decision even occurs. That is, Seizing is not a stage that always manifests as a visible decision-making event; under specific conditions, judgment can persist in a state where it remains within the organization without being translated into institutional decision. In this respect, this study raises the necessity of understanding Seizing not as a simple outcome-oriented act of choice, but as a processual capability that enables judgment to be converted into decision.",

    "717": "Upon this logical trajectory, this study thoroughly refuses to reduce the empirical findings derived through the foregoing Chapter 4 to merely a practical-level decision-making failure or an implementation-stage deficiency. Rather, it takes as its core analytical target the 'disconnected state' itself\u2014in which normative judgment was firmly gestated within the organizational ecosystem yet remained ossified without being linked to the structural network of formal institutional decision-making. In other words, the most decisive scholarly assertion pervading this inquiry lies in the fact that it has clearly confirmed, through field-level empirical data, that 'the fact of judgment's existence' and the substantive 'occurrence of decision' within the larger system are by no means processes sharing an identical trajectory.",

    "718": "The exposure of such structural disconnection emphatically demands departure from the inertia by which existing Dynamic Capabilities Theory has treated the Seizing phase as a one-off, singular act of strategic determination. Furthermore, it suggests that the 'totality of continuous flow'\u2014through which gestated judgment is forged into the complete language of formal decision via the institutional procedural network and the meticulous responsibility distribution structure within the organization\u2014must be wholly reset as an independent unit of analysis. Consequently, the deadlock witnessed in this case cannot be dismissed as a failure event of conventional Seizing capability; rather, it should properly be interpreted as a firm empirical foundation demonstrating that a vacuum state of 'non-activation'\u2014where the organizational conditions for driving judgment into substantive decision were never constructed in the first place\u2014can be preserved within an organization for a prolonged period.",

    "719": "In summary, the theoretical re-positioning conducted in this section corresponds to the point of departure for the entire discussion of this study. The case from Chapter 4 functions not as an exceptional failure case, but as empirical evidence demonstrating what state forms within an organization when the Seizing mechanism fails to operate. Through this shift in perspective, a theoretical foundation is laid in the following section for more precisely analyzing the structural characteristics and operating mechanisms of the process that failed to transition from judgment to decision.",

    "720": "5.2. Structural Characteristics of the Failed Judgment-to-Decision Process",

    "721": "The purpose of this section is to depart from existing explanatory frameworks that reduce the reasons why organizational-level judgment failed to transition into formal decision-making\u2014the core phenomenon confirmed in Chapter 4 and Section 5.1\u2014to individual attitudes or deficiencies in implementation will, and instead to re-position this phenomenon as a matter of structural operating conditions inherent within the organization. To this end, this section examines the organizational conditions typically required for judgment to transition into decision after its formation, and systematically analyzes that in this case, such conditions were not merely absent but that the decision process itself was structured and maintained in a state of non-initiation.",

    "722": "The analysis of this section is conducted on the basis of the operational indicators established in Chapter 3. That is, the formation of judgment and the status of decision conversion were not determined by abstract impressions or post-hoc interpretation, but were confirmed through concrete operational indicators including formal agenda-setting status, explicit designation of responsible agents, schedule establishment, procedure initiation, and the existence of electronic approval system records. Accordingly, the 'structural void' discussed in this section is not an interpretive inference but a structural analysis grounded in the repeated absence of conversion indicators observed at the operational indicator level.",

    "723": "In this study, 'judgment' does not denote an individual's immediate choice or subjective perception; rather, it is defined as a collective process in which specific information, possibilities, or problem recognition are recognized, interpreted, and legitimated as objects of strategic consideration at the organizational level, at the pre-decision stage. That judgment has been formed means that the pertinent possibility has acquired the institutional qualification to become an object of discussion, review, deferral, or adoption within the organization, but it does not yet signify that a formal decision has been made.",

    "724": "Generally, for judgment to convert into decision within an organization, several minimal structural devices must operate in sequence. First, a responsible agent must be clearly designated to translate the formed judgment into formal decision-making procedures. Second, discussion schedules or review timelines must be established so that the judgment is incorporated into the organization's timetable. Third, a procedural pathway must be initiated to concretize the judgment into actionable alternatives. Fourth, the attribution of authority and responsibility must be institutionally settled so that this process can be actually advanced. In prior Seizing research, these conditions have generally been tacitly presupposed; however, the case of this study empirically demonstrates that, when these presuppositions are not met, judgment can stagnate without converting into decision.",

    "725": "In this case, despite the sharing of judgment regarding the necessity of AI utilization within the organization and the formation of a certain level of consensus, none of the structural conditions presented above officially operated. No agent was designated to place the judgment on the formal agenda, and neither meeting schedules nor decision-making procedures for discussing the matter were established. Moreover, the initial-stage institutional procedures for linking judgment to the implementation stage\u2014such as procedural document preparation, budget review, and responsible department assignment\u2014were themselves never initiated.",

    "726": "Consequently, the gestated judgment was preserved within the organizational ecosystem as a meaningful recognition; nevertheless, the procedural transit channel for propelling it onto the trajectory of substantive formal decision was never opened. The normative judgment firmly existed, yet the institutional mediating devices that should have driven it into the full decision-making system were left in a thoroughly deactivated vacuum state.",

    "727": "It is not tenable to dismiss such a deadlock pattern as a transient administrative error or accidental delay on the part of working-level staff. The most decisive blind spot captured from the field through this inquiry lies not in the fact that a particular matter was artificially rejected in mid-progress, but in the point that the procedural initiation toward decision-making was fundamentally sealed from the outset. In other words, the essential limitation originates not from 'a decision containing errors' but from the 'situation of non-initiation of the procedure required to forge a decision' itself. This clearly demonstrates that the trajectory of transition from gestated judgment to decision is by no means a domain that is mechanically and automatically guaranteed; rather, it is a rigorous 'conditional process' that is activated only when specific structural prerequisites are fully conjoined.",

    "728": "A point that must be examined with particular acuity at this juncture is the fact that no documentary trace of officially rejecting or wholly negating the raised matter was observed. This absence of conflict and resistance signifies, beyond surface-level tranquility, that even under conditions where inter-member confrontation is entirely absent, if the structural devices to trigger conversion do not awaken, substantive decision may never be gestated. In conclusion, the evaporation of decision-making is not necessarily an inevitable product of internal resistance; rather, it is an autonomous phenomenon that can take root in an extremely stable form within the organizational ecosystem when a vacuum state\u2014in which the minimum conditions for conversion are not met\u2014becomes ossified over a prolonged period.",

    "729": "Upon this logical foundation, this section thoroughly excludes any attempt to reduce the cause of the discontinuity\u2014whereby gestated judgment failed to transition into full decision\u2014to the passive attitudes of specific practitioners or to abstract organizational culture. Instead, it directs its sharp analytical focus to the fact that the macro-level, structural elements that should have accompanied the phase following the settlement of judgment entirely failed to interlock in the field. That is, it rigorously demonstrates that the derivation of decision was not simply deferred, but that the institutional framework for conversion\u2014the very skeleton of transition\u2014was never constructed in the first place. Such a new analytical perspective emphatically urges a wholesale redefinition: departing from the inertia that has treated the Seizing phase of Dynamic Capabilities as a one-off act of strategic determination, it calls for reconceiving Seizing as a 'processual structure' that awakens only when the pluralistic elements of clarification of responsibility loci, establishment of temporal trajectories, procedural transit pathways, and substantive distribution of authority are tightly combined.",

    "730": "The state in which such essential structural conditions are left absent does not merely signify the delay of physical time. Rather, it constructs an obstinate terrain of 'non-decision' in which firmly gestated judgment floats in the deep strata of the organization without ever being translated into the language of formal decision.",

    "731": "In comprehensive assessment, the fatal disconnection between judgment and decision empirically confirmed in this research subject cannot be dismissed as an accidental exceptional circumstance produced by transient factors. Rather, it should properly be treated as a 'structural phenomenon' that, when the essential conversion conditions guaranteeing the operation of the Seizing stage are absent, can be repeatedly varied and reproduced within any organizational ecosystem. Such a refined theoretical interpretation provides the firmest theoretical premise for elucidating the processual operating mechanisms of the obstinate non-decision state to be dissected in the following section.",

    "733": "5.3. Processual Dynamics of the Non-decision State",

    "734": "The purpose of this section is to analyze the state in which, despite the formation of judgment, formal decision-making did not occur\u2014not as a simple resultant failure or temporary delay, but as a processual state that is stably maintained and reproduced under specific conditions within the organization. As confirmed in the analyses of Chapter 4 and Section 5.2, in this case, no indicators of decision conversion\u2014such as designation of responsible agents, schedule setting, or procedure initiation\u2014were observed in the post-judgment stage. This phenomenon needs to be understood not as 'a result of decision failure,' but as the outcome of the process of transitioning to decision itself not having been institutionally invoked for a sustained period.",

    "735": "The operational indicators established in the preceding Chapter 3 function as the firm analytical criteria pervading this section. In determining whether gestated judgment leapt onto the trajectory of substantive decision, the researcher's arbitrary interpretation was thoroughly excluded. Instead, only objectively observable empirical indicators\u2014including the history of formal agenda-setting, clear establishment of implementation schedules, assignment of responsible agents, and records of follow-up procedure initiation\u2014were cross-verified. Consequently, the fact that such signals of institutional conversion were persistently absent from the field data powerfully demonstrates that the 'non-decision state' designated by this study is by no means a product of abstract conjecture, but a processual pattern proven at a thoroughly empirical level.",

    "736": "The first core attribute defining the non-decision phase is the so-called 'invisibility of stagnation.' This refers to the peculiar terrain in which, although no substantive follow-up measure whatsoever was activated after the settlement of normative judgment, the deadlock situation itself entirely failed to surface as a problem within the organization\u2014precisely because neither overt resistance nor a formal rejection decision existed. In the actual field site under investigation, operational indicators proving the leap toward decision-making were entirely absent, yet no documentary trace of wholly denying or suppressing the matter was found either. Ultimately, the fatal discontinuity of 'the void of decision' was quietly dissolved into the inertia of routine administration without ever being called out as an organizational failure. This empirically demonstrates that the elimination of surface-level conflict does not directly translate into the advancement of institutional procedures.",

    "737": "The second indicator captured is encapsulated as 'organizational erasure of time.' This designates the phase in which, despite judgment being safely gestated within the larger system, it was not anchored into the temporal orbit through which the organization operates\u2014such as concrete deadlines or official meeting schedules\u2014thus fundamentally losing procedural momentum toward the future. Examining the subject case, while a broad consensus regarding the task of AI technology adoption was formed, the institutional timetable for formally addressing or ultimately resolving it was never constructed. When evaluated by the rigorous criterion of operational indicators, this void of deadlines is not a transient lapse by working-level staff but a phenomenon proving that the formed judgment thoroughly failed to settle within the organization's formal temporal structure. Matters that thus fail to enter orbit are inevitably pushed to the periphery of the decision-making system and ultimately solidify into a 'floating state that can be retrieved at any time but is never definitively settled.'",

    "738": "The third core mechanism manifests as 'absence of accountable ownership.' Examining the field trajectory, tacit support and positive sentiments among internal members toward the agenda did exist; however, the dedicated department or responsible agent who should have grasped this and ushered it into the arena of formal decision-making to drive follow-up planning was institutionally never called upon. When diagnosed through the lens of the previously established operational indicators, the 'omission of responsible agent assignment records' is a profoundly painful point, clearly demonstrating that the authority distribution mechanism\u2014which should have metamorphosed the gestated judgment into concrete organizational action\u2014was thoroughly paralyzed. As an inevitable consequence, the subject agenda remained trapped at the level of insubstantial collective consensus and ultimately failed to leap onto the trajectory of full decision-making.",

    "739": "The fourth prominent characteristic is 'non-activation of procedural thresholds.' This denotes a vacuum state in which the minimum institutional gateways that the gestated judgment must necessarily traverse to be translated into the language of substantive decision\u2014for instance, agenda-setting, formal documentation, and official review processes\u2014were not artificially rejected, but the basic conditions for initiating such systems were never fundamentally established. The point that must be most acutely honed analytically here is not the intentional 'blocking' of the matter, but the thorough 'non-calling' of institutional devices. The empirical clues collected from this single case are not products of overt resistance; rather, they constitute the stark fact itself that the operational indicators expected to prove signs of conversion uniformly maintained silence. In sum, the procedural system clearly existed within the organization, yet no one activated it, and consequently the formed judgment was unable to find a path forward, sinking into a deep quagmire of deadlock.",

    "740": "The four characteristics derived above are by no means fragmented phenomena existing in isolation or in parallel. They successively link tail-to-tail and construct a highly 'self-reinforcing processual loop' in which they firmly buttress one another. When judgment fails to enter the organization's formal temporal network (No Timetable), no clear accountability can be distributed to dedicate and drive it forward (No Accountable Ownership); under conditions where the responsible agent has evaporated, the procedural threshold toward decision-making is never invoked (Procedural Non-activation); and as long as the procedural gears remain halted, the present grave deadlock situation is not surfaced as any official problem (Invisibility of Stagnation). Within such a thoroughly concealed stagnation phase, there is no room even to raise the justification for deadline confirmation or authority delegation; ultimately, the terrain of non-decision coils within the organization in an extremely stable form, nourished by this fierce circular loop.",

    "741": "In conclusion, the landscape of non-decision pervading the organizational interior is not an accidental gap produced by transient factors after the gestation of judgment. Rather, it should properly be apprehended as an obstinate 'structural state' that is ceaselessly varied and reproduced in the deep strata of the larger system when the essential conversion devices linking judgment to substantive decision fail to conjoin. This insight empirically demonstrates that the Seizing phase of Dynamic Capabilities Theory is not a passive process that mechanistically operates in response to environmental change, but rather one that reveals itself only when meticulously constructed temporal trajectories, clear delegation of authority, and procedural transit pathways\u2014the essential mediating devices\u2014are awakened.",

    "742": "In comprehensive assessment, the non-decision state cannot be dismissed as merely 'an empty vacuum in which no event whatsoever occurred (non-event).' Rather, it is a highly dynamic 'processual mechanism' itself, in which firmly established judgment persists tenaciously within the organizational ecosystem without being translated into the language of full institutional decision. When the four axes of erasure of time, void of authority, non-activation of thresholds, and concealment of stagnation interlock to complete a massive circular loop, such a deadlock phenomenon far transcends accidental delay and becomes ossified as an ironclad organizational pattern. This meticulously elucidated self-reinforcing circular structure provides the firmest analytical foundation for the highly refined elaboration of the fatal process discontinuity in the Seizing stage and the decisive absence of 'institutional triggers' to be dissected in the immediately following section.",
}

